course: everyday
lesson: 286
title: Being trusted and what to do with things you find
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Trust is like a glass vase: quick to break and slow to mend. If you lied before and your parents now check up on you more, the way back is to keep telling the truth, day after day, until trust grows again.
explain: You earn trust in small jobs: bringing back the right change and receipt from the shop, behaving well when the teacher steps out, watching a younger sibling, looking after a lent book, being a fair class monitor, or watering a neighbour's plants every day without touching anything else.
explain: «Finders keepers, losers weepers» is a playground rhyme, not a rule. Something lost still belongs to its owner, who did not give it away. The rule is to make a real effort to find the owner.
explain: Example: a girl in Edinburgh finds a fiver in a second-hand coat. She shows her mum, who takes it back to the charity shop. A tiny coin on an empty pavement is different: there is no way to find its owner, so a few cents or pennies may be kept, but bigger finds should be handed in.
explain: Being fair also means not using what you should not have: if you find tomorrow's test answers, don't look, and give them to the teacher.
fact: Trust is quick to lose and slow to rebuild; keep telling the truth and it will grow back slowly.
fact: When sent to the shop, bring back the right change and the receipt; being careful with money shows you can be trusted.
fact: Behaving well when no one is watching is what makes you trustworthy.
fact: When looking after a younger sibling, keep them in sight and call an adult if anything happens.
fact: Take care of things people lend you and return them on time; it shows respect.
fact: In a job like class monitor, be fair and don't favour your friends.
fact: When trusted with a neighbour's home, do the job as agreed and don't touch other things.
fact: «Finders keepers» is a rhyme, not a rule; a lost thing still belongs to its owner.
fact: If a machine gives you extra by mistake, tell the shop or school, because someone else paid for it.
fact: If you find answers to a test, don't look and give them to the teacher; using them would be cheating.
fact: Money found in a second-hand book or coat belongs to someone else; ask an adult what to do.
fact: Very small sums with no way to find the owner may be kept, but bigger finds should be handed in.
fact: Encourage friends to return things that belong to classmates; it makes a class where people trust each other.
fact: Share fairly and offer to others first; fairness keeps friendships strong.
exercise: You lied to your parents last month, and now they check your homework every night. What helps most? | Keep telling the truth, and trust will grow back slowly | Lie better so they don't notice | Get angry because they don't trust you | Promise once, and expect full trust from them at once the next day
exercise: Why is trust slow to rebuild? | It is quick to lose and needs many honest days to grow back | Parents never forget anything, so it can't grow back at all | Trust only comes back on birthdays | It never can be rebuilt at all
exercise: Your dad sends you to the shop with $20 for milk. What should you bring back? | The milk, the right change and the receipt | The milk, plus some sweets you buy with the change for yourself | Just the milk; the change is yours | Nothing but a story about prices
exercise: The teacher leaves the classroom for a few minutes. What shows you are trustworthy? | Behaving as if she were still there | Playing games on her computer at her desk | Throwing paper about while she's out of the room | Leaving the room to go and look for her
exercise: You are asked to watch your little brother for five minutes. What is right? | Keep him in sight and call an adult if anything happens | Play a video game in another room and check on him later | Leave him to play near the road | Go out to buy him a snack
exercise: A friend lends you her favourite book. What is right? | Take care of it and return it on time | Lend it on to someone else in your class | Keep it until she forgets she ever lent it to you | Use it as a coaster for your cups of tea
exercise: You are class monitor for the day, handing out the new pencils. What is fair? | Hand them out fairly, not favouring friends | Give the best ones to your friends | Keep the best one for yourself | Give extras to whoever shouts the loudest and asks most often
exercise: A neighbour in Calgary asks you to water her plants while she is away. What is right? | Water them every day as agreed and touch nothing else | Water them once and hope it rains | Look in her drawers and cupboards while you are there alone | Invite friends over to her house
exercise: Someone shouts «Finders keepers, losers weepers!» about a found ball. What is true? | It's a rhyme, not a rule; the ball still belongs to its owner | It is a real rule in law that lets you keep anything that you find | It means the loser must pay you | It only applies to toys
exercise: The vending machine at school gives you two snacks instead of one. What is right? | Tell the school, because the extra was paid for by someone else | Keep both, because it was the machine's mistake and not yours at all | Share the extra one with your friends | Try again to get more
exercise: You find tomorrow's test answers on the photocopier. What do you do? | Don't look, and give them to the teacher | Read them quickly and put them back so nobody knows | Share them with your friends | Take a photo for later
exercise: You find a $10 bill in a second-hand coat. What is right? | Ask an adult; it may be returned or donated | Spend it quickly before anyone asks | Hide it in your money box | Put it back in the pocket and forget all about it
exercise: You find a single penny on an empty pavement with nobody around. What is the sensible view? | A very small sum with no way to find the owner may be kept, after telling an adult | You must put up posters all over town about the penny | You must give it to the police | It must be left exactly where it is on the ground, in case the owner ever comes back for it
exercise: Why should a bigger find, like a wallet, be handed in? | It is easier to find the owner, and the rule is to really try | Wallets are always worth nothing | Only police can open wallets | So that you get a big reward from the owner every time
exercise: A friend wants to keep a pencil case he found, but you know it is Ella's. What do you do? | Encourage him to give it back to her | Help him hide it | Say nothing, it's not your business | Tell him to sell it
exercise: You are sharing a bag of sweets with three friends. What is fair? | Share them equally and offer to others first | Keep the best ones for yourself | Give the most to whoever is loudest and asks again and again | Share only with your best friend
test: A boy who broke a promise wants his mum to trust him again tomorrow. Which is TRUE? | Trust grows back slowly with many days of honesty | One apology brings back full trust at once, the very same day | Trust can never come back | Trust comes back if he buys a present
test: A girl helps the teacher by marking spelling tests and sees her best friend got three wrong. What is fair? | Mark them all honestly, the same for everyone | Mark her friend's as right | Let her friend fix them first before handing them back in | Give her friend extra points
test: Your neighbour gave you a key to feed his cat. You notice his new games console. What is right? | Feed the cat and leave everything else alone | Play the console a little | Invite a friend to play too | Borrow a game and bring it back later before he gets home
test: You find a phone on a park bench in Dublin. What is the best thing to do? | Give it to an adult so they can find the owner | Keep it, because «finders keepers», and that's the rule | Use it until someone calls | Leave it on the ground nearby
test: A shop gives you too much change by mistake. What is right? | Point out the mistake and give the extra back | Keep it, because it was their mistake and not your fault | Spend it on sweets at once | Give it to a friend
test: Why does it matter how you behave when no adult is watching? | That is what really shows you are trustworthy | It doesn't matter if no one sees | Only behaviour in front of grown-ups counts | Cameras will report you anyway
test: Your little sister is playing in the paddling pool while you mind her, and your friend calls you to come and see something inside. What should you do? | Stay and keep her in sight | Go off quickly, she'll be fine | Tell her to get out and go alone | Ask her to shout if she slips
test: A friend lent you a game and asked for it back on Friday. What shows respect? | Returning it on Friday in good condition | Keeping it until you finish the game yourself | Lending it to your cousin for the weekend | Returning it some time next month instead
test: A boy finds a test answer sheet and says «It's not cheating if I just found it». What is true? | Using it would still be cheating and unfair to the class | It is fine because he did not steal it, he only found it lying there | It is fine if he only looks once | It is only cheating if he shares it
test: found a fiver on the pavement, finders keepers yeah? | No, it still belongs to someone, ask an adult | Yes, that's the rule everyone uses, it's yours | Yes, as long as nobody saw you pick it up off the floor | Only if it's worth less than ten pounds in total
test: vending machine gave me 2 drinks at school | Tell the school, someone else paid for that | Keep it, the machine made the mistake, not you | Share the extra one with your best mate at break | Put it back in the slot of the machine again
test: found the test answers on teachers desk 👀 | Don't look, give them to the teacher | Peek just at the hard ones | Share them with the whole class | Take a photo for later
test: babysitting my lil bro, gonna go play upstairs | Keep him in sight and call an adult if anything happens | Fine, he'll shout up the stairs if he needs you | Fine, if you check every hour | Lock him in his room to be safe
explain: A girl in Florida was left to watch her little brother by the pool and went inside for a minute to get a drink; he slipped in, and only her mum's quick swim saved him.
explain: A boy in Leeds kept a wallet he found in the park; it held an old man's only money for the week, and the police later traced it back to him.
fact: A small child can drown in under a minute, quietly, without splashing or shouting.
fact: Keeping something you know belongs to someone else can count as theft, even if you found it.
test: watching my little bro, need the loo for a sec | Take him with you or call an adult first | Leave him, it's only a second and he'll be fine | Tell him to sit still | Lock him in his room
test: found a phone on the bus, finders keepers yeah | No, hand it to the driver or the lost property | Yes, it's yours now, the owner should've been careful | Yes, if nobody saw you | Yes, if it's not locked
test: lied to mum last week now she checks everything i do | Keep telling the truth, trust comes back slowly | Lie better so she won't notice | Stop talking to her | Tell her she has to trust you again right now
test: did u hear a kid found a wallet with "like 300 dollars" and kept it, then the police turned up at school. lesson? | Hand in things you find; keeping them can count as theft | Finders keepers, it's the rule, so the police can't do anything | Keep it if nobody saw you | Only hand in wallets with ID
test: my cousin says the boy babysitting his little brother "went on his phone and the baby fell down the stairs and nearly died". what's the true bit to learn | Keep a younger sibling in sight and call an adult if anything happens | Babies never fall when someone's home | Phones help you watch kids better | Little ones are fine alone for an hour if the door is locked
test: kids say the class monitor "only picks her mates, she's a cheat". pass it on? | No, don't spread it; tell her or a teacher calmly if it's unfair | Yes, everyone should know she cheats | Yes, and get the whole class to ignore her until she stops | Yes, and post it on the class chat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the rhyme «finders keepers»? | It is a rhyme and not a rule, since a lost item still belongs to its owner | It is a rule recognised in law | It applies to all items found outdoors, though not those found indoors | It applies to any sum of money found
exam: Identify the principal reason for which extra items dispensed by a machine in error are to be reported. | Someone else paid for them | Machines report such errors automatically | The items are invariably defective | Reporting them earns a reward
exam: It is given that a pupil comes upon the answers to a forthcoming test. Select the response consistent with honesty. | The pupil shall not look at them and shall give them to the teacher | The pupil may study them carefully provided that nobody else does | The pupil shall share them equally with the class | The pupil shall return them after reading them once
exam: State which of the following describes how trust behaves once it has been lost. | It is quick to lose and slow to rebuild through continued truthfulness | It is restored at once by a single apology | It can never be regained under any circumstances, however honest one is | It returns automatically after one week
